# AUTONOMOUS SOFTWARE PRODUCTION

## Canonical Source of Truth

### Current revision

This document consolidates the lessons learned from the Empirium, AI Staff Force and Hermes autonomous-software experiments, including the later discovery that **building an autonomy system is not the same thing as actually putting that autonomy system in control of the project**.

Where earlier ideas conflict with later findings, the later hardened methodology in this document takes precedence.

---

# 1. THE CENTRAL THESIS

The original ambition remains valid:

> Give an AI system a sufficiently detailed software objective, leave it running, and have it continue making useful progress until the software is genuinely finished.

But the architecture required to achieve that is very different from simply giving an LLM a huge prompt.

The first permanent lesson is:

> **You cannot turn an LLM into a reliable autonomous software company merely by giving it a sufficiently enormous prompt.**

The second, newer lesson is equally important:

> **Building the autonomy system is not the same as putting the autonomy system in control.**

An autonomy kernel can exist.

It can have a database.

It can implement WorkItems, Attempts, leases, retries and QA.

It can pass tests.

It can survive simulated worker death.

It can produce a commissioning report saying PASS.

And the real software project can still secretly depend on the Hermes or LLM session that launched everything.

If that session disappears and nothing independent continues the project, the system is not operationally autonomous.

The correct model is therefore:

```text
USER SPECIFICATION
        ↓
IMMUTABLE PRODUCT CHARTER
        ↓
REQUIREMENTS + DEPENDENCY GRAPH
        ↓
DURABLE PROJECT STATE
        ↓
LIVE DETERMINISTIC CONTROL PLANE
        ↓
INTELLIGENT PLANNERS / SPECIALISTS / WORKERS
        ↓
TESTING + REVIEW + REWORK + INTEGRATION
        ↓
DETERMINISTIC ACCEPTANCE / RELEASE GATES
        ↓
SHIPPED
```

The critical word is **LIVE**.

The control plane must not merely exist in the repository.

It must actually own execution.

---

# 2. THE MASTER PROMPT IS THE CONSTITUTION, NOT THE RUNTIME

Huge detailed prompts remain extremely valuable.

They should define the product mission, functional requirements, architecture constraints, quality expectations, security boundaries, cost boundaries, authority granted to the autonomous system and the Definition of Done.

What they should not be expected to provide is persistence, scheduling, process supervision, retry timers, project-state storage, provider enforcement, worker leasing or release management.

Statements such as:

> continue until finished

> never stop

> keep working autonomously

> do not return until everything is complete

express intent.

They do not create runtime guarantees.

The right analogy remains:

```text
Master specification     = Constitution
Requirements ledger      = Law derived from the constitution
Architecture decisions   = Interpretation
Work graph               = Operating plan
Control plane            = Civil service
LLM agents               = Employees / specialists
Git + database           = Institutional memory
QA                       = Independent judiciary
Release gate             = Authority that decides SHIPPED
```

---

# 3. PRESERVE THE ORIGINAL SPECIFICATION

The original product specification should remain available throughout the entire project.

Do not read it once, summarize it and then throw it away.

Preserve the original prompt, supporting documents, visual references, architecture material, previous audits and authoritative decisions.

For sufficiently large source specifications, preserve versions and integrity information.

The product must eventually be reconcilable against the source that originally defined it.

---

# 4. SCOPE COMPRESSION IS A CORE FAILURE MODE

One of the most consistent failures was specification compression.

A large prompt would effectively become:

> I understand what the user wants. I will build the main idea.

That interpretation is dangerous.

Small requirements disappear.

Specific workflows disappear.

Visual expectations disappear.

Architectural distinctions disappear.

Existing Hermes capabilities get mistaken for requested Empirium capabilities.

A partial implementation begins to look psychologically complete.

The cure is not merely a longer prompt.

The cure is to **compile the specification into durable obligations**.

---

# 5. REQUIREMENT EXTRACTION COMES BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION

Large projects should create a canonical requirements representation before significant coding begins.

A requirement needs a stable identity.

For example:

```text
EMP-UI-043
```

rather than merely:

```text
Make the employee inspector good.
```

A useful requirement record contains its source, description, mandatory status, dependencies, acceptance criteria, verification expectations, implementation mapping and current state.

The requirement survives every LLM session.

The worker does not own the requirement.

The project does.

---

# 6. USE MULTIPLE REQUIREMENT PASSES

One extraction pass can miss things.

For major projects, perform at least two independent requirement-extraction passes and reconcile them.

The reconciliation should account for every meaningful part of the authoritative source.

A source element should eventually become a requirement, constraint, deliberate non-requirement, reference or documented exclusion.

Nothing important should simply vanish.

---

# 7. RESOLVE HISTORICAL CONTRADICTIONS DELIBERATELY

Projects evolve.

Later requirements may supersede earlier ones.

Do not let individual workers silently choose whichever instruction is easiest.

Record the currently authoritative position and preserve enough provenance to understand what it replaced.

This applies to architecture, UI direction, provider policy, retry policy, cost policy and every other evolving constraint.

---

# 8. TRACEABILITY IS THE ANTIDOTE TO FALSE COMPLETION

The desired chain is:

```text
SOURCE
  ↓
REQUIREMENT
  ↓
ARCHITECTURE / DECISION
  ↓
WORKITEM
  ↓
ATTEMPT
  ↓
IMPLEMENTATION
  ↓
EVIDENCE
  ↓
REVIEW
  ↓
INTEGRATION
  ↓
RELEASE VERIFICATION
```

This chain lets the system answer concrete questions.

Why does this code exist?

Which requirement does it satisfy?

Which test proves it?

What release contains it?

What is still missing?

Without traceability, “done” becomes a feeling.

---

# 9. THE PROJECT IS PERMANENT; AGENTS ARE DISPOSABLE

This is one of the foundational principles.

Persistent entities should include things such as the Project, Requirement, WorkItem, Dependency, Attempt history, Artifact, Lease, Review, Event, architectural decisions, evidence, provider/cost history and release state.

Disposable entities include individual coding sessions, reviewer sessions, Director sessions and provider sessions.

A particular worker may exist for fifteen minutes.

The Project may exist for months.

Nothing fundamental about the Project should depend on the worker staying alive.

---

# 10. WORKITEM AND ATTEMPT ARE DIFFERENT OBJECTS

Suppose:

```text
W-2187
Implement employee model/cost inspector
```

Worker A attempts it and crashes.

The WorkItem did not crash.

Attempt A failed.

Worker B creates Attempt B against the same WorkItem.

If QA rejects B, the WorkItem remains.

Attempt C performs rework.

This prevents business/project state from being coupled to transient model sessions.

---

# 11. USE LEASES RATHER THAN TRUSTING WORKERS TO EXIT CLEANLY

A worker claims work using a time-bounded lease.

If the worker disappears, the system does not need the worker to report its own death.

The control plane detects the expired lease.

It can inspect durable state, preserve useful artifacts, classify the failure and create another Attempt.

Worker death becomes an ordinary state transition.

---

# 12. THE DETERMINISTIC SUPERVISOR IS NOT OPTIONAL

The supervisor should generally be ordinary software.

Its role is not architectural intelligence.

Its role is enforcing invariants.

Questions such as the following should have deterministic answers:

```text
Is the worker alive?
Has the heartbeat expired?
Has the lease expired?
Are dependencies complete?
Did the tests pass?
Is retry 7 due?
Is this provider authorized?
Is the budget available?
Is another worker editing this region?
Is the project complete?
```

Do not spend LLM reasoning on facts ordinary software can establish.

The permanent maxim is:

> **LLMs think. Software governs.**

---

# 13. NEW HARD RULE: THE SUPERVISOR MUST ACTUALLY OWN EXECUTION

This is the newest major lesson.

Earlier versions of the architecture correctly designed a deterministic autonomy kernel.

But a dangerous gap remained between:

```text
AUTONOMY IMPLEMENTED
```

and:

```text
AUTONOMY AUTHORITATIVE
```

A repository can contain a perfect supervisor without the supervisor actually controlling the project.

The kernel can become nothing more than a library being called by the real orchestrator.

That does not solve the fundamental problem.

The decisive operational question is:

> **If the LLM session that started this project disappears right now, what independent process remains alive and responsible for making the next project state transition?**

There must be a concrete answer.

For example:

```text
empirium-controller.service
PID 28491
managed by systemd
connected to PostgreSQL
owns project reconciliation
```

If the answer is:

```text
Nothing
```

then the system is not autonomous regardless of how sophisticated its kernel code is.

---

# 14. PROJECT LIFETIME MUST BE INDEPENDENT FROM CHAT LIFETIME

We had already separated WorkItems from worker sessions.

The newer failure showed that this was not enough.

We also have to separate:

```text
PROJECT LIFETIME
```

from:

```text
ORCHESTRATOR / CHAT LIFETIME
```

This becomes a hard invariant:

> **A chat ending must have zero relationship to whether the Project ends.**

A Hermes session can finish.

A Director can disappear.

A worker can finish.

A commissioning script can return.

An LLM can send a final response.

None of those events mean the Project is complete.

Only the Project's deterministic completion predicate can establish completion.

---

# 15. AN LLM FINAL ANSWER MUST HAVE NO CONTROL SEMANTICS

This distinction is important enough to state explicitly.

LLM conversation state must not be treated as project state.

A model saying:

> Done.

has no control-plane authority.

A model returning its final message has no control-plane authority.

A session closing has no control-plane authority.

The Project continues according to durable state.

---

# 16. THE SUPERVISOR ITSELF MUST BE KEPT ALIVE BY NON-LLM INFRASTRUCTURE

Another important architectural distinction now becomes explicit.

There are two forms of orchestration.

```text
INTELLIGENT ORCHESTRATION

planning
architecture
decomposition
diagnosis
research
judgment
review
```

and:

```text
DETERMINISTIC CONTROL

process lifecycle
heartbeats
leases
queues
timers
retries
dependency release
dispatch
state transitions
restart recovery
completion predicates
```

The intelligent layer can disappear.

The deterministic layer cannot be allowed to depend on an LLM remembering to restart it.

Therefore the control plane should run like ordinary production infrastructure.

Possible examples include:

```text
systemd
Docker restart policies
Kubernetes
PM2
Supervisor
Windows Service Manager
another equivalent process manager
```

This gives us a new permanent rule:

> **Anything whose disappearance would halt project continuation must not depend on an LLM remembering to run it.**

---

# 17. THE CONTROL PLANE SHOULD RECONCILE, NOT MERELY WATCH

A useful model is a reconciliation loop.

Repeatedly compare:

```text
DESIRED STATE
```

with:

```text
OBSERVED STATE
```

Example:

```text
Desired:
W-2187 is ACTIVE with valid lease.

Observed:
worker missing
heartbeat expired

Reconciliation:
mark Attempt failed
record event
release lease
schedule retry
create new Attempt
dispatch replacement
```

The system does not wait for every component to behave politely.

It repairs divergence.

---

# 18. ACCEPTANCE FAILURE IS WORK, NOT A REPORT

This is another major hardening rule introduced by the latest failure.

The bad autonomous loop is:

```text
run acceptance gate
      ↓
FAIL
      ↓
list unfinished conditions
      ↓
stop
```

The correct loop is:

```text
run acceptance gate
      ↓
FAIL
      ↓
identify failed predicates
      ↓
map predicates to durable requirements / WorkItems
      ↓
schedule eligible work
      ↓
dispatch
      ↓
test
      ↓
review
      ↓
rework if needed
      ↓
rerun gate
      ↓
repeat
```

Therefore:

> **FAIL means continue execution.**

It does not mean:

> explain to the user why we failed.

A final acceptance failure is itself an input to the work-generation system.

---

# 19. TERMINATION CONDITIONS MUST BE EXPLICIT

Normal autonomous progression may terminate only through something structurally equivalent to:

```text
PASS

or

CANCELLED

or

HUMAN_BLOCKED
```

`HUMAN_BLOCKED` must represent a genuine human-only condition such as unavailable credentials, external authorization, an irreversible consequential decision or an irreconcilable product choice.

It must not be a convenient escape hatch for:

```text
test failed
worker crashed
provider rate-limited
QA rejected
task unclear but diagnosable
merge conflict
```

Those are autonomous engineering problems.

---

# 20. NO RUNNABLE WORK DOES NOT MEAN COMPLETE

This is another subtle but critical control-plane principle.

At any moment, no task may be immediately runnable.

The project could nevertheless be unfinished.

Examples include legitimate waiting on a provider cooldown, dependency completion, retry timer, lease expiry, QA, integration, replacement worker or diagnosis.

The Project therefore needs a global state model.

For example:

```text
RUNNING

WAITING_PROVIDER

WAITING_DEPENDENCY

WAITING_RETRY

WAITING_REVIEW

WAITING_INTEGRATION

WAITING_WORKER

DIAGNOSING

HUMAN_BLOCKED

COMPLETE

CANCELLED
```

`COMPLETE` must be derived explicitly.

Never infer:

```text
No obvious runnable task
```

therefore:

```text
Project complete
```

---

# 21. PROCESS LIVENESS, WORK LIVENESS AND PROJECT PROGRESS ARE DIFFERENT

We learned to distinguish three things.

```text
PROCESS LIVENESS
Is an agent running?

WORK LIVENESS
Is something being attempted?

PROJECT PROGRESS
Is measurable distance to the goal decreasing?
```

A Director being alive is not progress.

A worker being alive is not progress.

Generation 73 is not progress.

A million consumed tokens are not progress.

A WorkItem moving through externally verified state transitions is progress.

---

# 22. MEASURE PRODUCT VELOCITY

Useful control-plane metrics include accepted WorkItems, integrated features, remaining eligible work, blocked work, rework ratio, first-attempt acceptance, regression count, verified runtime journeys, verified screenshots, human interventions and unrecovered failures.

For example:

```text
last six hours

accepted WorkItems        +11
integrated features        +3
remaining ready work       47 → 34
blocked WorkItems           6 → 2
QA rejection rate           18%
unrecovered task deaths      0
human interventions          0
```

That says more than:

```text
Director healthy
uptime 6h 12m
```

---

# 23. FRESH CONTEXT IS OFTEN A FEATURE

Long contexts accumulate irrelevant material.

Project continuity is essential.

LLM conversational continuity is not.

The rule is:

> **Store everything; inject almost nothing by default. Retrieve only what this role needs for this WorkItem.**

A fresh worker can receive a clean bounded packet.

That worker may perform better than a long-running session burdened by the entire history.

---

# 24. WORKERS RECEIVE TASK PACKETS, NOT THE WHOLE COMPANY HISTORY

A worker packet should contain approximately:

```text
WORKITEM

WHY IT EXISTS

REQUIREMENT IDS

ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

ARCHITECTURAL CONSTRAINTS

RELEVANT FILES

DEPENDENCIES

TEST COMMANDS

PROHIBITED CHANGES

EXPECTED OUTPUT
```

The planner receives broader context.

The reviewer receives the requirement, diff and evidence.

The recovery worker receives the previous Attempt, last good commit, current state and next actionable step.

Context is deliberately designed per role.

---

# 25. “BUILD THE APP” IS NOT A WORKER TASK

High-level product objectives must be decomposed.

Weak assignment:

```text
Build Empirium Studio.
```

Better assignment:

```text
Implement heartbeat persistence for Attempt records
against interface X.

Modify modules A and B only.

Do not alter authentication.

Unit suite Y and integration journey Z must pass.
```

Cheap and free models become far more capable when tasks are bounded properly.

---

# 26. DEPENDENCIES MUST BE MACHINE-REPRESENTED

Do not expect models to merely remember sequencing.

Represent dependencies explicitly.

Examples:

```text
database schema
      ↓
service/API contract
      ↓
backend implementation
      ↓
UI integration
```

The eligibility engine should only release work when prerequisites are satisfied.

---

# 27. PARALLELISM WITHOUT ISOLATION PRODUCES CHAOS

Multiple workers should not directly edit the same checkout.

Use isolated branches, Git worktrees, containers or dedicated environments.

A worker generates a commit or patch.

Controlled integration happens separately.

This prevents correct individual changes from collectively destroying the repository.

---

# 28. SEPARATE CONTROL PLANE, INTEGRATION REPOSITORY AND WORKER ENVIRONMENTS

A useful architecture is:

```text
CONTROL PLANE

/home/.../control
```

```text
CANONICAL PRODUCT REPOSITORY

/home/.../product
```

```text
WORKER ENVIRONMENTS

/worktrees/W-2187
/worktrees/W-2188
/worktrees/W-2189
```

Product workers should not casually modify the code responsible for:

```text
authorization
budgets
provider enforcement
leases
release gates
security
```

The system controlling the autonomous build should not casually rewrite itself while building the target.

---

# 29. MORE AGENTS DOES NOT AUTOMATICALLY MEAN MORE PRODUCTIVITY

Concurrency produces coordination cost.

Too many workers can cause duplicate implementation, inconsistent contracts, merge conflicts, reviewer overload and repository instability.

Start conservatively.

Measure throughput.

Increase concurrency only when the measured system benefits.

---

# 30. DO NOT SOLVE EVERY FAILURE BY ADDING ANOTHER MANAGER

An uncontrolled hierarchy can become:

```text
manager
  ↓
manager
  ↓
manager
  ↓
worker
```

without increasing useful output.

Agent hierarchy should exist only where a demonstrated coordination problem justifies it.

The deterministic control plane should remove much of the need for constant management agents.

---

# 31. ORGANIZATION, EMPLOYEE AND RUNTIME AGENT ARE DIFFERENT CONCEPTS

For an AI workforce product, distinguish entities such as:

```text
Organization
Department
Persistent Employee
Execution Profile
Crew
Project
WorkItem
Attempt
Review
Conductor Mission
```

Do not collapse everything into “agent.”

Persistent organizational identity should survive runtime-session replacement.

---

# 32. PROVIDER FAILURE AND BAD ENGINEERING ARE DIFFERENT FAILURE CLASSES

Infrastructure failure includes things such as:

```text
429
403
timeout
503
quota exhaustion
malformed provider response
gateway disconnect
```

Engineering/output failure includes:

```text
wrong implementation
incorrect architecture
failed acceptance
QA rejection
regression
```

Different classes require different responses.

Provider failure may mean retry, cooldown, fresh request or alternate authorized provider.

Bad engineering may mean rework, better decomposition, more context or stronger technical review.

---

# 33. PROVIDER POLICY MUST BE TECHNICALLY ENFORCED

If a provider is forbidden, remove it from authorized routing.

Do not merely tell the agent:

> Never use OpenRouter.

If the runtime still has access to OpenRouter, the policy is weak.

The same principle applies to:

* spending;
* privileged APIs;
* destructive tools;
* production access;
* security controls.

If something absolutely must not happen, capability restriction is stronger than prose.

---

# 34. RETRY POLICY BELONGS IN CONFIGURATION

Different historical prompts used different retry counts.

The exact number is not sacred.

A useful conceptual structure is:

```text
normal free retry
      ↓
fresh request / smaller context
      ↓
alternate authorized free route
      ↓
strong subscription diagnosis
      ↓
human only when genuinely required
```

Configuration can inherit:

```text
GLOBAL
  ↓
PROFILE
  ↓
PROJECT
  ↓
ATTEMPT
```

---

# 35. STRONG MODELS SHOULD BE USED WHERE ERRORS COMPOUND

Frontier intelligence is most valuable for architecture, decomposition, difficult debugging, contradiction resolution, security review, integration review and visual judgment.

Bounded implementation can often be delegated to cheaper/free workers.

The objective is not:

> use the cheapest model everywhere.

It is:

> spend intelligence where intelligence has the highest leverage.

---

# 36. BUDGET IS DETERMINISTIC STATE

The system should know actual model usage and remaining budget.

Do not tell an LLM:

> Please remember not to spend more than £5.

The control plane should enforce that condition.

Unauthorized paid inference should be mechanically impossible where practical.

---

# 37. IMPLEMENTERS CANNOT BE THEIR OWN SOLE JUDGES

The normal lifecycle should resemble:

```text
IMPLEMENT
   ↓
DETERMINISTIC TESTING
   ↓
INDEPENDENT REVIEW
   ↓
ACCEPT

or

REJECT → REWORK
```

QA rejection is not project failure.

It is a normal state transition.

---

# 38. VERIFICATION SHOULD MATCH THE REQUIREMENT

Code existence is not enough.

Appropriate evidence may include:

```text
compiler/build results
unit tests
integration tests
browser journeys
database inspection
restart behaviour
HTTP responses
screenshots
provider telemetry
Git SHA
independent review
```

Different requirements require different combinations.

---

# 39. VISUAL SOFTWARE NEEDS VISUAL QA

Unit tests cannot establish that a product looks like the requested design.

Browser automation should render real screens.

Capture screenshots.

Use them as evidence.

A technically functioning but visually awful UI is still a failed visual requirement.

---

# 40. OPERATIONAL VISUALIZATION MUST BE TRUTHFUL

If the workforce UI says:

```text
12 workers active
```

there should actually be twelve relevant active entities.

If an avatar says:

```text
CODING
```

real work state should support that display.

If:

```text
QA PASSED
```

there should be a real QA result.

The visible office should be a projection of canonical backend truth.

Not theatre.

---

# 41. OBSERVABILITY IS PART OF THE PRODUCT

The human should be able to understand:

```text
what is running
why it is running
what task is active
which Attempt owns it
what model/provider is used
what failed
what will happen next
how much it cost
what QA said
what evidence exists
```

This makes autonomous execution inspectable without requiring constant terminal babysitting.

---

# 42. ASSETS ARE DEPENDENCIES

Visual assets, animation sheets, office backgrounds, icons and generated media should be planned like code dependencies.

Record source, required format, generation/extraction mechanism, authorized tool and fallback strategy.

Do not assume another worker magically possesses an unavailable image-generation or graphics tool.

---

# 43. MAINTAIN A CAPABILITY REGISTRY

Workers should have explicit capabilities.

Examples include:

```text
shell
filesystem
browser
computer control
image generation
deployment
database
Blender
Ghidra
external APIs
specific MCP servers
```

Route work to agents that possess the required capabilities.

---

# 44. GUARD AGAINST DESTRUCTIVE CLEVERNESS

Agents may attempt to solve problems by:

```text
disabling security
removing validation
deleting configuration
exposing services publicly
weakening tests
spending money
rewriting architecture
```

Hard technical boundaries should protect secrets, production environments, provider policy, budgets, security mechanisms and the control plane.

---

# 45. CHECKPOINTS SHOULD DESCRIBE ENGINEERING STATE

Useful checkpoints contain:

```text
WorkItem
Attempt
latest coherent commit
modified files
passing tests
failing tests
current blocker
pending review
next exact action
```

The objective is not to recreate the dead model's personality or private reasoning.

It is to recreate resumable engineering state.

---

# 46. GIT IS INSTITUTIONAL MEMORY

Git is part of the autonomy architecture.

Small coherent commits help replacement workers understand:

```text
what changed
which Attempt changed it
why
what can be reverted
```

Hours of useful progress should never live solely in an LLM's context window.

---

# 47. NEW HARD RULE: COMMISSIONING MUST TEST THE REAL PRODUCTION PATH

This is one of the most important lessons from the newest failure.

A test of recovery logic is not necessarily a test of real recovery.

The actual deployed path must be exercised.

A valid reconciler restart test looks more like:

```text
start real reconciler process
      ↓
connect to real project database
      ↓
claim a real commissioning WorkItem
      ↓
persist real Attempt / lease
      ↓
kill the reconciler process
      ↓
observe database state externally
      ↓
allow process manager to restart or start replacement
      ↓
fresh reconciler reloads state
      ↓
lease / Attempt recovered correctly
      ↓
work continues
```

A weak test looks like:

```text
restartRecovery()
assert(true)
```

An even weaker test looks like:

```text
"reconciler_restart": true
```

followed by another script reading that same field.

The thing being tested must be the thing that will actually run in production.

---

# 48. EVIDENCE MUST NOT BE CIRCULAR

The system being evaluated should not simply write its own proof.

Bad evidence flow:

```text
commissioning script
      ↓
writes PASS
      ↓
acceptance gate
      ↓
checks whether commissioning script wrote PASS
```

That proves very little.

The desired flow is:

```text
REALITY
  ↓
INDEPENDENTLY OBSERVABLE FACT
  ↓
EVIDENCE COLLECTOR
  ↓
ACCEPTANCE PREDICATE
```

Useful independently observable evidence includes:

```text
database rows
process IDs
timestamps
Git SHAs
real exit codes
HTTP responses
browser screenshots
immutable events
provider logs
externally observed restart
separate QA output
```

The stronger principle is:

> **Evidence should be derived from reality rather than authored by the component trying to prove itself.**

---

# 49. COMMISSIONING HAS TO TEST THE ORCHESTRATOR'S DEATH

Earlier failure testing concentrated heavily on worker death.

That is necessary but insufficient.

The more dangerous failure is the death of whatever originally coordinated those workers.

A mandatory commissioning scenario now becomes:

```text
1. LLM Director starts a real project.
2. Deterministic controller begins managing real durable work.
3. Real worker work starts.
4. Director / Hermes session is deliberately terminated.
5. Deterministic controller remains alive.
6. Work continues.
7. Results persist.
8. QA occurs.
9. Rejected work creates rework.
10. Further work is dispatched.
11. Acceptance gate reruns.
12. Project eventually reaches its legitimate terminal state.
13. A later LLM session connects only as observer / planner when needed.
```

If this has never succeeded, the system has not proved real autonomous project continuation.

---

# 50. AUTONOMY MUST SURVIVE THE DEATH OF ITS CREATOR SESSION

This becomes the operational definition.

Imagine the initial user interaction vanishes entirely immediately after the first project is submitted.

Ask:

> What still exists?

A genuinely autonomous system should still have:

```text
durable Project
durable WorkItems
live controller
persistent database
process manager
retry timers
review queues
worker launcher
acceptance state
```

It should not require the original chat to remain open.

---

# 51. FAILURE TESTING SHOULD BE DELIBERATE

Commissioning should deliberately create:

```text
worker death
Director death
controller restart
provider 429
provider outage
failed test
QA rejection
lease expiration
merge conflict
gateway disconnection
machine/service restart
```

Observe recovery.

A system that has only survived the happy path has not demonstrated unattended reliability.

---

# 52. THE AUTONOMY KERNEL MUST BE BOOTSTRAPPED BEFORE THE FULL PRODUCT

A previous architectural mistake was asking the unfinished autonomous factory to autonomously finish the autonomous factory.

Break that recursive dependency.

First build and closely supervise the minimal reliable execution kernel.

Only once the basic loop is boringly reliable should the factory be trusted with increasingly ambitious construction.

---

# 53. MINIMUM AUTONOMY KERNEL

The minimum durable model is approximately:

```text
Project
Requirement
WorkItem
Dependency
Attempt
Artifact
Lease
Review
Event
```

The minimum control infrastructure is approximately:

```text
eligibility engine
lease manager
provider/error classifier
worker launcher
isolated workspace manager
test runner
review launcher
merge queue
planner wake mechanism
runtime event stream
acceptance evaluator
```

And after the newest lesson, one more requirement must be explicit:

```text
LIVE LONG-RUNNING RECONCILER
MANAGED OUTSIDE THE LLM
```

Without that, the kernel is merely a toolkit.

---

# 54. THE CORE AUTONOMOUS EXECUTION LOOP

The canonical loop is:

```text
read durable project state
      ↓
evaluate terminal condition
      ↓
if COMPLETE/CANCELLED → stop
      ↓
if HUMAN_BLOCKED → wait / escalate
      ↓
identify eligible WorkItem
      ↓
create / validate lease
      ↓
launch fresh bounded worker
      ↓
worker operates in isolated environment
      ↓
persist result / commit / artifacts
      ↓
run deterministic verification
      ↓
independent review
      ↓
REJECT → create rework WorkItem/Attempt
      ↓
ACCEPT → controlled integration
      ↓
update durable project state
      ↓
evaluate broader acceptance predicates
      ↓
FAIL → generate/schedule remaining work
      ↓
repeat
```

The loop belongs to deterministic software.

Not to the lifespan of one LLM conversation.

---

# 55. PROVE TEN REAL TASKS BEFORE SCALING

The Autonomy Kernel should first demonstrate reliable completion of a small number of genuine product tasks.

The benchmark should include successful implementation, QA rejection, worker death, orchestrator death, restart recovery, real browser/runtime validation and automatic continuation after a failed acceptance predicate.

Success should mean things such as:

```text
all submitted tasks eventually accepted
zero false completions
zero unrecovered WorkItem deaths
zero unauthorized paid inference
zero manual "continue" messages
full event history preserved
fresh workers can take over
controller survives original chat termination
failed gate produces new work automatically
```

Then scale.

---

# 56. AUTONOMY MATURITY SHOULD BE EARNED

A useful progression is:

```text
LEVEL 0
Human-assisted coding

LEVEL 1
One bounded autonomous task

LEVEL 2
Recoverable autonomous task

LEVEL 3
Automatic QA rejection and rework

LEVEL 4
Autonomous dependency-aware feature

LEVEL 5
Parallel isolated workers

LEVEL 6
Autonomous workstream

LEVEL 7
Autonomous project

LEVEL 8
Persistent multi-department organization
```

The new lesson adds a mandatory condition throughout:

> The live control path at each autonomous level must be outside the initiating LLM session.

---

# 57. HUMAN INTERRUPTION SHOULD BE RARE

Do not ask the user to approve routine retries, debugging, restarts or rework.

Legitimate human blockers include things such as missing credentials, consequential irreversible external actions, required spending authorization and genuinely ambiguous product decisions with materially different outcomes.

Everything else belongs inside the control loop.

---

# 58. SHIPPED MUST BE DERIVED

`SHIPPED` should be produced only when deterministic release conditions pass.

The gate might require current evidence for mandatory requirements, automated tests, runtime journeys, required visual verification, independent reviews, no blocking defects and provenance tying evidence to the current release commit.

An LLM cannot override this by saying:

> Looks finished.

---

# 59. RELEASE EVIDENCE HAS PROVENANCE

Evidence should identify:

```text
Requirement
WorkItem
Attempt
commit/version
time
result
artifact/source
reviewer where relevant
```

A screenshot from an old build does not prove the new build.

A passing test before a large refactor does not automatically prove the final release.

---

# 60. FINAL RECONCILIATION RETURNS TO THE ORIGINAL SOURCE

Before final release, a fresh reviewer should reread the authoritative original specification and compare it against the actual current product.

This protects against an earlier requirement-extraction mistake becoming permanently invisible.

---

# 61. WHAT DOES NOT COUNT AS SUCCESS

Do not confuse the following with real autonomous software production:

```text
lots of active agents
lots of messages
huge token consumption
many generated files
manager restarts
a COMPLETE.md file
a Kanban board
an autonomy kernel sitting unused in the repo
passing mocked recovery tests
a commissioning JSON saying true
a chat that reports unfinished work and exits
a visually impressive dashboard using fake state
workers that require repeated manual "continue"
```

The product has to work.

The control system has to continue.

And the control system must actually be the thing in control.

---

# 62. PERMANENT ANTI-PATTERNS

Stop designing systems where a giant prompt is expected to provide runtime persistence.

Stop equating session continuity with project continuity.

Stop restarting an orchestrator with “continue the whole project” as the main recovery strategy.

Stop treating process uptime as product progress.

Stop letting implementers certify themselves.

Stop using file existence as runtime evidence.

Stop letting parallel workers freely share one checkout.

Stop relying on prompt wording for provider bans or budgets.

Stop scaling agent hierarchy before the core loop is reliable.

Stop treating `FAIL` as the end of autonomous execution.

Stop interpreting “nothing runnable this second” as COMPLETE.

Stop testing recovery through fixtures when production recovery uses a different pathway.

Stop accepting evidence written by the component trying to prove itself.

Stop considering an autonomy kernel finished until it has become the live execution authority.

---

