# 64. CANONICAL OPERATING PROCEDURE FOR FUTURE PROJECTS

The standard methodology is now:

```text
Preserve the original specification.

Inspect the actual repository and environment.

Perform independent requirement extraction passes.

Reconcile requirements and contradictions.

Create stable requirement IDs.

Define architecture and foundational contracts.

Create dependency-aware WorkItems.

Deploy the deterministic controller as a real persistent service.

Verify that the controller—not the initiating chat—is the authoritative execution path.

Commission the real production execution pathway.

Deliberately kill workers.

Deliberately kill the Director.

Deliberately restart the controller.

Verify state recovery against the real durable database.

Verify that failed acceptance predicates generate new WorkItems.

Verify QA rejection produces rework.

Verify provider failures create correct waiting/retry behaviour.

Verify "nothing runnable" does not become COMPLETE.

Verify evidence comes from independently observable facts.

Run bounded workers in isolated environments.

Continuously test implementation.

Perform independent QA.

Integrate accepted work.

Measure accepted product progress.

Perform milestone visual/runtime QA.

Reread the original source against the real product.

Run deterministic final acceptance.

Only PASS may produce SHIPPED.
```

---

# 65. THE HARD DEFINITION OF “AUTONOMOUS”

From now on, we should not describe a software-building system as genuinely autonomous unless this statement is true:

> **After the initiating LLM session has been deliberately terminated, a non-LLM-managed persistent control process can independently reconstruct project state, identify the correct next action or legitimate wait condition, dispatch further work when appropriate, recover failures, process QA and rework, rerun acceptance, and eventually reach a legitimate terminal state without the user typing “continue.”**

That is now the commissioning standard.

---

# 66. CANONICAL PRINCIPLES

**Principle 1 — The specification is persistent; the LLM is disposable.**

**Principle 2 — The Project survives every chat, worker, planner and reviewer.**

**Principle 3 — LLMs think; software governs.**

**Principle 4 — Building the controller does not mean the controller is in control.**

**Principle 5 — The live execution authority must exist outside the initiating LLM.**

**Principle 6 — Anything whose disappearance would halt the Project must not depend on an LLM remembering to keep it alive.**

**Principle 7 — A final chat response has no relationship to Project completion.**

**Principle 8 — FAIL creates work; it does not conclude the Project.**

**Principle 9 — No runnable task is a scheduling state, not a completion predicate.**

**Principle 10 — Evidence must come from observable reality rather than self-authored claims.**

**Principle 11 — Commissioning must exercise the exact production pathway.**

**Principle 12 — Kill the orchestrator during commissioning.**

**Principle 13 — WorkItems persist; Attempts are replaceable.**

**Principle 14 — Store everything; inject only the context needed for the current job.**

**Principle 15 — Explicit dependencies outperform assumed sequencing.**

**Principle 16 — Parallelism requires isolation.**

**Principle 17 — Implementers cannot be their own sole reviewers.**

**Principle 18 — Provider restrictions, budgets and safety rules must be technical controls.**

**Principle 19 — Runtime and visual evidence matter as much as static code.**

**Principle 20 — Process liveness is not project progress.**

**Principle 21 — Measure accepted product outcomes rather than agent activity.**

**Principle 22 — Recovery must be proven through destructive commissioning tests.**

**Principle 23 — Complexity is earned only after the simpler autonomous loop works.**

**Principle 24 — SHIPPED is a derived state, never an opinion.**

---

# 67. THE TWO SENTENCES TO PRESERVE ABOVE EVERYTHING ELSE

The first lesson from the earlier experiments was:

> **Do not try to make the agent persistent enough to finish the software. Make the software project persistent enough that disposable agents can finish it.**

The new failure adds its necessary companion:

> **Do not merely build the system that could autonomously run the project. Make that system the actual live authority running the project, then prove the project continues after the LLM that created it is gone.**

Together, those two statements capture the architecture we have been trying to discover.